INT. APPELLATE COURTROOM - ANTEROOM - DAY

Wood panelling. Water pitcher. A window onto a car park.

ATTORNEY CONSTANCE ABIOLA-HARTWELL, 50s, checks her notes. Her opposing counsel, ASHER DELACROIX-NWOSU, 40s, enters, nods, and takes the other end of the table.

She doesn't look up. He sets down his briefcase.

CONSTANCE
(to the room, loud)
Everyone should be seated by nine.

No one else is there. Asher raises an eyebrow.

ASHER
Talking to the furniture?

CONSTANCE
Habit.

He sits. She shuffles pages. He does the same. Their hands brush a stapler at the same instant. Both withdraw.

ASHER
After you.

CONSTANCE
No. You.

He picks it up. Staples. Stares at the result.

ASHER
(muttering, to himself)
Wrong page.

She notices. Says nothing. The clock ticks.

A buzzer sounds. They both rise, grab their briefcases, and collide at the door. She goes first. He follows.

CONSTANCE
Don't rush me.

ASHER
Wouldn't dream of it.

They enter the courtroom side by side, not touching.
